meta {
  name: Delete Campaign Zip Resolution
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

delete {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/platform/mailer-zip-markets/{{campaignZipMarketId}}
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{platformAccessToken}}
}

tests {
  test("removes the row, or reports it was never there", function () {
    // Two acceptable answers, by design — see the docs block. 200 when the
    // campaign folder ran first and wrote the resolution; 404 when this folder
    // was run on its own.
    expect([200, 404]).to.include(res.getStatus());
  });
}

docs {
  # DELETE /platform/mailer-zip-markets/:id — undo the campaign's inline resolution

  Same endpoint as `Delete Zip Market`; see there for the contract.

  ## Why this exists

  `Platform Mailer Campaigns/Patch Zip Resolutions` resolves ZIP `99999` mid-run
  and the API **persists that answer** to this table, which is the whole point of
  the resolver flow: the operator answers once and every campaign after it
  benefits. That also means the campaign folder's own assertion — *"99999 is
  surfaced as unmatched"* — is only true the first time it runs.

  Removing the row here restores the starting state, so the collection stays
  re-runnable. The CLI walks folders alphabetically, so `Platform Mailer
  Campaigns` has already read and asserted on it by the time this runs.

  ## Why it accepts a `404`

  Run this folder on its own and the row was never written. A `404` is then the
  correct answer and the collection should still go green — the same trade
  `Users/Resend Invite` makes with its cooldown `409`.
}
